{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongrui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dongrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zecheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The LLM Already Knows: Estimating LLM-Perceived Question Difficulty via Hidden Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:38:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:36:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Estimating the difficulty of input questions as perceived by large language models (LLMs) is essential for accurate performance evaluation and adaptive inference. Existing methods typically rely on repeated response sampling, auxiliary models, or fine-tuning the target model itself, which may incur substantial computational costs or compromise generality. In this paper, we propose a novel approach for difficulty estimation that leverages only the hidden representations produced by the target LLM. We model the token-level generation process as a Markov chain and define a value function to estimate the expected output quality given any hidden state. This allows for efficient and accurate difficulty estimation based solely on the initial hidden state, without generating any output tokens. Extensive experiments across both textual and multimodal tasks demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms existing baselines in difficulty estimation. Moreover, we apply our difficulty estimates to guide adaptive reasoning strategies, including Self-Consistency, Best-of-N, and Self-Refine, achieving higher inference efficiency with fewer generated tokens."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12883","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12883","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12883","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qifei","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Qifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yajie","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Yajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintong","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xintong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiming","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Qiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yasen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Runyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoquan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guoquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lego-Edit: A General Image Editing Framework with Model-Level Bricks and MLLM Builder"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:36:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Instruction-based image editing has garnered significant attention due to its direct interaction with users. However, real-world user instructions are immensely diverse, and existing methods often fail to generalize effectively to instructions outside their training domain, limiting their practical application. To address this, we propose Lego-Edit, which leverages the generalization capability of Multi-modal Large Language Model (MLLM) to organize a suite of model-level editing tools to tackle this challenge. Lego-Edit incorporates two key designs: (1) a model-level toolkit comprising diverse models efficiently trained on limited data and several image manipulation functions, enabling fine-grained composition of editing actions by the MLLM; and (2) a three-stage progressive reinforcement learning approach that uses feedback on unannotated, open-domain instructions to train the MLLM, equipping it with generalized reasoning capabilities for handling real-world instructions. Experiments demonstrate that Lego-Edit achieves state-of-the-art performance on GEdit-Bench and ImgBench. It exhibits robust reasoning capabilities for open-domain instructions and can utilize newly introduced editing tools without additional fine-tuning.\n Code is available: https://github.com/xiaomi-research/lego-edit."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12883","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:48Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12876","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12876","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12876","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuyu","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Fuyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zimu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zimu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking and Improving LVLMs on Event Extraction from Multimedia Documents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:29:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:36:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of multimedia content necessitates the development of effective Multimedia Event Extraction (M2E2) systems. Though Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have shown strong cross-modal capabilities, their utility in the M2E2 task remains underexplored. In this paper, we present the first systematic evaluation of representative LVLMs, including DeepSeek-VL2 and the Qwen-VL series, on the M2E2 dataset. Our evaluations cover text-only, image-only, and cross-media subtasks, assessed under both few-shot prompting and fine-tuning settings. Our key findings highlight the following valuable insights: (1) Few-shot LVLMs perform notably better on visual tasks but struggle significantly with textual tasks; (2) Fine-tuning LVLMs with LoRA substantially enhances model performance; and (3) LVLMs exhibit strong synergy when combining modalities, achieving superior performance in cross-modal settings. We further provide a detailed error analysis to reveal persistent challenges in areas such as semantic precision, localization, and cross-modal grounding, which remain critical obstacles for advancing M2E2 capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at INLG 2025. Camera-ready version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12876","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:38Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yabo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yabo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qingyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kavin","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kavin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wangmeng","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Wangmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tool-R1: Sample-Efficient Reinforcement Learning for Agentic Tool Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:22:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities in language understanding and reasoning, yet they remain limited when tackling real-world tasks that require up-to-date knowledge, precise operations, or specialized tool use. To address this, we propose Tool-R1, a reinforcement learning framework that enables LLMs to perform general, compositional, and multi-step tool use by generating executable Python code. Tool-R1 supports integration of user-defined tools and standard libraries, with variable sharing across steps to construct coherent workflows. An outcome-based reward function, combining LLM-based answer judgment and code execution success, guides policy optimization. To improve training efficiency, we maintain a dynamic sample queue to cache and reuse high-quality trajectories, reducing the overhead of costly online sampling. Experiments on the GAIA benchmark show that Tool-R1 substantially improves both accuracy and robustness, achieving about 10\\% gain over strong baselines, with larger improvements on complex multi-step tasks. These results highlight the potential of Tool-R1 for enabling reliable and efficient tool-augmented reasoning in real-world applications. Our code will be available at https://github.com/YBYBZhang/Tool-R1."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:26Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12866","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12866","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12866","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Thißen","name":"Thißen, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thi Ngoc Diep","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Thi Ngoc Diep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barbara Esteve","familyName":"Ratsch","name":"Ratsch, Barbara Esteve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben Joel","familyName":"Schönbein","name":"Schönbein, Ben Joel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ute","familyName":"Trapp","name":"Trapp, Ute","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beate","familyName":"Egner","name":"Egner, Beate","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Romana","familyName":"Piat","name":"Piat, Romana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elke","familyName":"Hergenröther","name":"Hergenröther, Elke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Large Language Models to Effectively Generate Visual Data for Canine Musculoskeletal Diagnoses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:22:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.24132/csrn.2025-3","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is well-established that more data generally improves AI model performance. However, data collection can be challenging for certain tasks due to the rarity of occurrences or high costs. These challenges are evident in our use case, where we apply AI models to a novel approach for visually documenting the musculoskeletal condition of dogs. Here, abnormalities are marked as colored strokes on a body map of a dog. Since these strokes correspond to distinct muscles or joints, they can be mapped to the textual domain in which large language models (LLMs) operate. LLMs have demonstrated impressive capabilities across a wide range of tasks, including medical applications, offering promising potential for generating synthetic training data. In this work, we investigate whether LLMs can effectively generate synthetic visual training data for canine musculoskeletal diagnoses. For this, we developed a mapping that segments visual documentations into over 200 labeled regions representing muscles or joints. Using techniques like guided decoding, chain-of-thought reasoning, and few-shot prompting, we generated 1,000 synthetic visual documentations for patellar luxation (kneecap dislocation) diagnosis, the diagnosis for which we have the most real-world data. Our analysis shows that the generated documentations are sensitive to location and severity of the diagnosis while remaining independent of the dog's sex. We further generated 1,000 visual documentations for various other diagnoses to create a binary classification dataset. A model trained solely on this synthetic data achieved an F1 score of 88% on 70 real-world documentations. These results demonstrate the potential of LLM-generated synthetic data, which is particularly valuable for addressing data scarcity in rare diseases. While our methodology is tailored to the medical domain, the insights and techniques can be adapted to other fields."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12866","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:24Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12863","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12863","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12863","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhan","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Haozhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingsong","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhong","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Sartoretti","name":"Sartoretti, Guillaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRATE: a Graph transformer-based deep Reinforcement learning Approach for Time-efficient autonomous robot Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:19:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous robot exploration (ARE) is the process of a robot autonomously navigating and mapping an unknown environment. Recent Reinforcement Learning (RL)-based approaches typically formulate ARE as a sequential decision-making problem defined on a collision-free informative graph. However, these methods often demonstrate limited reasoning ability over graph-structured data. Moreover, due to the insufficient consideration of robot motion, the resulting RL policies are generally optimized to minimize travel distance, while neglecting time efficiency. To overcome these limitations, we propose GRATE, a Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL)-based approach that leverages a Graph Transformer to effectively capture both local structure patterns and global contextual dependencies of the informative graph, thereby enhancing the model's reasoning capability across the entire environment. In addition, we deploy a Kalman filter to smooth the waypoint outputs, ensuring that the resulting path is kinodynamically feasible for the robot to follow. Experimental results demonstrate that our method exhibits better exploration efficiency (up to 21.5% in distance and 21.3% in time to complete exploration) than state-of-the-art conventional and learning-based baselines in various simulation benchmarks. We also validate our planner in real-world scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12863","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:20Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enes","familyName":"Koktas","name":"Koktas, Enes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Rost","name":"Rost, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"State Aware Traffic Generation for Real-Time Network Digital Twins"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:17:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Digital twins (DTs) enable smarter, self-optimizing mobile networks, but they rely on a steady supply of real world data. Collecting and transferring complete traces in real time is a significant challenge. We present a compact traffic generator that combines hidden Markov model, capturing the broad rhythms of buffering, streaming and idle periods, with a small feed forward mixture density network that generates realistic payload sizes and inter-arrival times to be fed to the DT. This traffic generator trains in seconds on a server GPU, runs in real time and can be fine tuned inside the DT whenever the statistics of the generated data do not match the actual traffic. This enables operators to keep their DT up to date without causing overhead to the operational network. The results show that the traffic generator presented is able to derive realistic packet traces of payload length and inter-arrival time across various metrics that assess distributional fidelity, diversity, and temporal correlation of the synthetic trace."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:16Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonina C.","familyName":"Eldar","name":"Eldar, Yonina C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-Play Diffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"G.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:15:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a posterior sampling algorithm for the problem of estimating multiple independent source signals from their noisy superposition. The proposed algorithm is a combination of Gibbs sampling method and plug-and-play (PnP) diffusion priors. Unlike most existing diffusion-model-based approaches for signal separation, our method allows source priors to be learned separately and flexibly combined without retraining. Moreover, under the assumption of perfect diffusion model training, the proposed method provably produces samples from the posterior distribution. Experiments on the task of heartbeat extraction from mixtures with synthetic motion artifacts demonstrate the superior performance of our method over existing approaches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 1 figure, submitted to conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:12Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libao","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Libao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Theoretical Analysis on the Ability of Particle Swarm Optimization to Escape from Local Optimum"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:09:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As one of the most prominent swarm intelligence algorithms, particle swarm optimization (PSO) has been extensively applied to solve global optimization problems. The theoretical analysis on the ability of PSO to escape from local optimum (LO) is deeply intertwined with its global optimization performance, making it highly significant to investigate the underlying mechanisms that enable PSO to escape from LO and achieve global optimization. In this study, a rigorous mathematical analysis is presented to explore the ability and mechanisms of PSO to escape from LO. By modelling each search agent as a Markov chain, the probability of PSO escaping from LO within a finite number of iterations is calculated, and the behavior of agents prior to escaping from LO is analyzed. Through this theoretical analysis, two necessary and sufficient conditions are proposed to ensure that PSO escapes from LO in finite iterations with a probability 1: (a) the personal best (pbest) and global best (gbest) positions stagnate around different local optima, and (b) the inertial weight in PSO is equal to one. Upon these conditions, two potential behaviors of agents, namely inertial motion and oscillation, are proposed, and a state transition chain of the agent toward the global optimum is constructed. Additionally, the theoretical analysis reveals that increasing the distance between pbest and gbest, as well as the values of the learning factors in PSO, enhances the speed at which PSO escapes from LO. Finally, the conclusions drawn from the theoretical analysis are corroborated through numerical experiments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:05Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12851","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12851","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12851","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Cetim, LS2N, LS2N - équipe RoMas, LS2N - équipe PACCE"],"givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Lénat","name":"Lénat, Antoine","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Cetim"],"givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Cheminat","name":"Cheminat, Olivier","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LS2N - équipe RoMas, LS2N"],"givenName":"Damien","familyName":"Chablat","name":"Chablat, Damien","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LS2N, UR2, LS2N - équipe PACCE"],"givenName":"Camilo","familyName":"Charron","name":"Charron, Camilo","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Novel Skill Modeling Approach: Integrating Vergnaud's Scheme with Cognitive Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:09:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human-machine interaction is increasingly important in industry, and this trend will only intensify with the rise of Industry 5.0. Human operators have skills that need to be adapted when using machines to achieve the best results. It is crucial to highlight the operator's skills and understand how they use and adapt them [18]. A rigorous description of these skills is necessary to compare performance with and without robot assistance. Predicate logic, used by Vergnaud within Piaget's scheme concept, offers a promising approach. However, this theory doesn't account for cognitive system constraints, such as the timing of actions, the limitation of cognitive resources, the parallelization of tasks, or the activation of automatic gestures contrary to optimal knowledge. Integrating these constraints is essential for representing agent skills understanding skill transfer between biological and mechanical structures. Cognitive architectures models [2] address these needs by describing cognitive structure and can be combined with the scheme for mutual benefit. Welding provides a relevant case study, as it highlights the challenges faced by operators, even highly skilled ones. Welding's complexity stems from the need for constant skill adaptation to variable parameters like part position and process. This adaptation is crucial, as weld quality, a key factor, is only assessed afterward via destructive testing. Thus, the welder is confronted with a complex perception-decision-action cycle, where the evaluation of the impact of his actions is delayed and where errors are definitive. This dynamic underscores the importance of understanding and modeling the skills of operators."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12851","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:03Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:50:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12834","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12834","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12834","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imre","familyName":"Bakó","name":"Bakó, Imre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"László","familyName":"Pusztai","name":"Pusztai, László","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orest","familyName":"Pizio","name":"Pizio, Orest","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pressure dependent structure of neat liquid methanol, CH3OH: molecular dynamics simulations with various united atom type potentials"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:58:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Molecular dynamics computer simulations have been conducted on neat liquid methanol, using three different united atom (three site) interatomic potentials: TraPPE [J. Phys. Chem. B 105, 3093 (2001)], UAM-I [J. Mol. Liq. 323, 114576 (2021)] and OPLS/2016 [J. Chem. Phys. 145, 034508 (2016)]. The effects of pressure, between 1 bar and 6 kbar, have been evaluated on total scattering structure factors, partial radial distribution functions, as well as on collective characteristics such as ring size distributions and cluster size distributions. Agreement with experimental density is nearly quantitative for all the three force fields, and major trends observed for recent pressure dependent neutron diffraction data are reproduced qualitatively. The OPLS/2016 force field, in general, generates properties that are markedly different from results originating from the other potentials. Pressure effects are hardly noticeable on most partial radial distribution functions and on the distribution of the number of hydrogen bonded neighbours. On the other hand, collective structural properties, like cluster- and ring size distributions exhibit significant changes with increasing pressure: larger clusters become more numerous, whereas the number of cyclic clusters, i.e., rings, decrease. The self-diffusion coefficient decreases with increasing pressure, and the same is valid for the average lifetime of hydrogen bonds."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12834","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:39Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:49:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12831","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12831","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12831","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javeria","familyName":"Amir","name":"Amir, Javeria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farwa","familyName":"Attaria","name":"Attaria, Farwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mah","familyName":"Jabeen","name":"Jabeen, Mah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umara","familyName":"Noor","name":"Noor, Umara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zahid","familyName":"Rashid","name":"Rashid, Zahid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Lightweight Pipeline for Noisy Speech Voice Cloning and Accurate Lip Sync Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:55:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent developments in voice cloning and talking head generation demonstrate impressive capabilities in synthesizing natural speech and realistic lip synchronization. Current methods typically require and are trained on large scale datasets and computationally intensive processes using clean studio recorded inputs that is infeasible in noisy or low resource environments. In this paper, we introduce a new modular pipeline comprising Tortoise text to speech. It is a transformer based latent diffusion model that can perform high fidelity zero shot voice cloning given only a few training samples. We use a lightweight generative adversarial network architecture for robust real time lip synchronization. The solution will contribute to many essential tasks concerning less reliance on massive pre training generation of emotionally expressive speech and lip synchronization in noisy and unconstrained scenarios. The modular structure of the pipeline allows an easy extension for future multi modal and text guided voice modulation and it could be used in real world systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12831","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:49:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12825","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12825","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12825","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacopo","familyName":"Rodeschini","name":"Rodeschini, Jacopo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Tedesco","name":"Tedesco, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Finazzi","name":"Finazzi, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Otto","name":"Otto, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Fassò","name":"Fassò, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multivariate Low-Rank State-Space Model with SPDE Approach for High-Dimensional Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62M10, 62H11, 62M30, 62P12, 65C20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:50:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:33:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes a novel low-rank approximation to the multivariate State-Space Model. The Stochastic Partial Differential Equation (SPDE) approach is applied component-wise to the independent-in-time Matérn Gaussian innovation term in the latent equation, assuming component independence. This results in a sparse representation of the latent process on a finite element mesh, allowing for scalable inference through sparse matrix operations. Dependencies among observed components are introduced through a matrix of weights applied to the latent process. Model parameters are estimated using the Expectation-Maximisation algorithm, which features closed-form updates for most parameters and efficient numerical routines for the remaining parameters. We prove theoretical results regarding the accuracy and convergence of the SPDE-based approximation under fixed-domain asymptotics. Simulation studies show our theoretical results. We include an empirical application on air quality to demonstrate the practical usefulness of the proposed model, which maintains computational efficiency in high-dimensional settings. In this application, we reduce computation time by about 93%, with only a 15% increase in the validation error."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12825","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:26Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:49:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12821","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12821","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12821","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Zach","name":"Zach, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssef","familyName":"Haouchat","name":"Haouchat, Youssef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Unser","name":"Unser, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Statistical Benchmark for Diffusion Posterior Sampling Algorithms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:46:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:33:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a statistical benchmark for diffusion posterior sampling (DPS) algorithms for Bayesian linear inverse problems. The benchmark synthesizes signals from sparse Lévy-process priors whose posteriors admit efficient Gibbs methods. These Gibbs methods can be used to obtain gold-standard posterior samples that can be compared to the samples obtained by the DPS algorithms. By using the Gibbs methods for the resolution of the denoising problems in the reverse diffusion, the framework also isolates the error that arises from the approximations to the likelihood score. We instantiate the benchmark with the minimum-mean-squared-error optimality gap and posterior coverage tests and provide numerical experiments for popular DPS algorithms on the inverse problems of denoising, deconvolution, imputation, and reconstruction from partial Fourier measurements. We release the benchmark code at https://github.com/zacmar/dps-benchmark. The repository exposes simple plug-in interfaces, reference scripts, and config-driven runs so that new algorithms can be added and evaluated with minimal effort. We invite researchers to contribute and report results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12821","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:21Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:49:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12816","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12816","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12816","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel Wiebe","familyName":"Werner","name":"Werner, Axel Wiebe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Beskow","name":"Beskow, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Deichler","name":"Deichler, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gesture Evaluation in Virtual Reality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.5.1; H.5.2; I.2.10; I.3.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50, 68T07, 68U35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:35:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:33:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3686215.3688821","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gestures are central to human communication, enriching interactions through non-verbal expression. Virtual avatars increasingly use AI-generated gestures to enhance life-likeness, yet evaluations have largely been confined to 2D. Virtual Reality (VR) provides an immersive alternative that may affect how gestures are perceived. This paper presents a comparative evaluation of computer-generated gestures in VR and 2D, examining three models from the 2023 GENEA Challenge. Results show that gestures viewed in VR were rated slightly higher on average, with the strongest effect observed for motion-capture \"true movement.\" While model rankings remained consistent across settings, VR influenced participants' overall perception and offered unique benefits over traditional 2D evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Proceedings of the 26th International Conference on Multimodal Interaction (ICMI '24), ACM. Copyright 2024 ACM. Licensed under CC BY"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12816","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:14Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:49:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12810","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12810","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12810","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shicheng","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Shicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiqiang","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Kaiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengdong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chengdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinqi","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yinqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"H$^2$R: Hierarchical Hindsight Reflection for Multi-Task LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:30:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:32:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM)-based agents have shown strong potential in multi-task scenarios, owing to their ability to transfer knowledge across diverse tasks. However, existing approaches often treat prior experiences and knowledge as monolithic units, leading to inefficient and coarse-grained knowledge transfer. In this work, we propose a novel hierarchical memory architecture that enables fine-grained knowledge transfer by decoupling high-level planning memory from low-level execution memory. To construct and refine these hierarchical memories, we introduce Hierarchical Hindsight Reflection (H$^2$R), a mechanism that distills reusable and hierarchical knowledge from past agent-environment interactions. At test time, H$^2$R performs retrievals of high-level and low-level memories separately, allowing LLM-based agents to efficiently access and utilize task-relevant knowledge for new tasks.Experimental results across two benchmarks demonstrate that H$^2$R can improve generalization and decision-making performance, outperforming prior baselines such as Expel."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12810","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:05Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:49:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nenad","familyName":"Petrovic","name":"Petrovic, Nenad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukasz","familyName":"Mazur","name":"Mazur, Lukasz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alois","familyName":"Knoll","name":"Knoll, Alois","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Approach for Enhancing Maintainability of Automotive Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:17:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:32:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There are many bottlenecks that decrease the flexibility of automotive systems, making their long-term maintenance, as well as updates and extensions in later lifecycle phases increasingly difficult, mainly due to long re-engineering, standardization, and compliance procedures, as well as heterogeneity and numerosity of devices and underlying software components involved. In this paper, we explore the potential of Large Language Models (LLMs) when it comes to the automation of tasks and processes that aim to increase the flexibility of automotive systems. Three case studies towards achieving this goal are considered as outcomes of early-stage research: 1) updates, hardware abstraction, and compliance, 2) interface compatibility checking, and 3) architecture modification suggestions. For proof-of-concept implementation, we rely on OpenAI's GPT-4o model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:48:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:48:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:48:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linchun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Linchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianbiao","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Xianbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingquan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qingquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Double Helix Diffusion for Cross-Domain Anomaly Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:06:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:31:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual anomaly inspection is critical in manufacturing, yet hampered by the scarcity of real anomaly samples for training robust detectors. Synthetic data generation presents a viable strategy for data augmentation; however, current methods remain constrained by two principal limitations: 1) the generation of anomalies that are structurally inconsistent with the normal background, and 2) the presence of undesirable feature entanglement between synthesized images and their corresponding annotation masks, which undermines the perceptual realism of the output. This paper introduces Double Helix Diffusion (DH-Diff), a novel cross-domain generative framework designed to simultaneously synthesize high-fidelity anomaly images and their pixel-level annotation masks, explicitly addressing these challenges. DH-Diff employs a unique architecture inspired by a double helix, cycling through distinct modules for feature separation, connection, and merging. Specifically, a domain-decoupled attention mechanism mitigates feature entanglement by enhancing image and annotation features independently, and meanwhile a semantic score map alignment module ensures structural authenticity by coherently integrating anomaly foregrounds. DH-Diff offers flexible control via text prompts and optional graphical guidance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that DH-Diff significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods in diversity and authenticity, leading to significant improvements in downstream anomaly detection performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:48:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:48:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:48:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12780","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12780","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12780","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumitra","familyName":"Daptari","name":"Daptari, Soumitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurabh","familyName":"Dwivedi","name":"Dwivedi, Saurabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A study on state spaces in classical Banach spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 46A22, 46B10, 46B25, Secondary 46B20, 46B22","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:55:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:31:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $X$ be a real or complex Banach space. Let $S(X)$ denote the unit sphere of $X$. For $x\\in S(X)$, let $S_{x}=\\{x^*\\in S(X^*):x^*(x)=1\\}$. A lot of Banach space geometry can be determined by the `quantum' of the state space $S_{x}$. In this paper, we mainly study the norm compactness and weak compactness of the state space in the space of Bochner integrable function and $c_{0}$-direct sums of Banach spaces. Suppose $X$ is such that $X^*$ is separable and let $μ$ be the Lebesgue measure on $[0,1]$. For $f\\in L^1(μ,X)$, we demonstrate that if $S_{f}$ is norm compact, then $f$ is a smooth point. When $μ$ is the discrete measure, we show that if $ (x_i) \\in S(\\ell^{1}(X))$ and $ \\|x_{i}\\|\\neq 0$ for all $i\\in{\\mathbb{N}}$, then $ S_{(x_i)}$ is weakly compact in $ \\ell^\\infty(X^*) $ if and only if $ S_{\\frac{x_i}{\\|x_i\\|}} $ is weakly compact in $X^*$ for each $i\\in{\\mathbb{N}}$ and $\\text{diam}\\left(S_{\\frac{x_i}{\\|x_i\\|}}\\right) \\to 0 $. For discrete $c_{0}$-sums, we show that for $(x_{i})\\in c_{0}(X)$, $S_{(x_{i})}$ is weakly compact if and only if for each $i_{0}\\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $\\|x_{i_{0}}\\|=1$, the state space $S_{x_{i_{0}}}$ is weakly compact."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in Colloquium Mathematicum"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12780","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:48:19Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:48:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:48:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12776","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12776","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12776","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Renjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangke","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Shangke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Integrating Trajectory Optimization and Reinforcement Learning for Quadrupedal Jumping with Terrain-Adaptive Landing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:46:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:30:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Jumping constitutes an essential component of quadruped robots' locomotion capabilities, which includes dynamic take-off and adaptive landing. Existing quadrupedal jumping studies mainly focused on the stance and flight phase by assuming a flat landing ground, which is impractical in many real world cases. This work proposes a safe landing framework that achieves adaptive landing on rough terrains by combining Trajectory Optimization (TO) and Reinforcement Learning (RL) together. The RL agent learns to track the reference motion generated by TO in the environments with rough terrains. To enable the learning of compliant landing skills on challenging terrains, a reward relaxation strategy is synthesized to encourage exploration during landing recovery period. Extensive experiments validate the accurate tracking and safe landing skills benefiting from our proposed method in various scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IROS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12776","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:48:14Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:48:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:48:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12765","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12765","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12765","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huangyu","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Huangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingtao","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Lingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyi","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Chenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InfoGain-RAG: Boosting Retrieval-Augmented Generation via Document Information Gain-based Reranking and Filtering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:29:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a promising approach to address key limitations of Large Language Models (LLMs), such as hallucination, outdated knowledge, and lacking reference. However, current RAG frameworks often struggle with identifying whether retrieved documents meaningfully contribute to answer generation. This shortcoming makes it difficult to filter out irrelevant or even misleading content, which notably impacts the final performance. In this paper, we propose Document Information Gain (DIG), a novel metric designed to quantify the contribution of retrieved documents to correct answer generation. DIG measures a document's value by computing the difference of LLM's generation confidence with and without the document augmented. Further, we introduce InfoGain-RAG, a framework that leverages DIG scores to train a specialized reranker, which prioritizes each retrieved document from exact distinguishing and accurate sorting perspectives. This approach can effectively filter out irrelevant documents and select the most valuable ones for better answer generation. Extensive experiments across various models and benchmarks demonstrate that InfoGain-RAG can significantly outperform existing approaches, on both single and multiple retrievers paradigm. Specifically on NaturalQA, it achieves the improvements of 17.9%, 4.5%, 12.5% in exact match accuracy against naive RAG, self-reflective RAG and modern ranking-based RAG respectively, and even an average of 15.3% increment on advanced proprietary model GPT-4o across all datasets. These results demonstrate the feasibility of InfoGain-RAG as it can offer a reliable solution for RAG in multiple applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP'25 Oral Presentation. Contact: benchen4395@gmail.com"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12765","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:59Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:47:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12754","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12754","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12754","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saki","familyName":"Hashimoto","name":"Hashimoto, Saki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoichi","familyName":"Hasegawa","name":"Hasegawa, Shoichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomochika","familyName":"Ishikawa","name":"Ishikawa, Tomochika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akira","familyName":"Taniguchi","name":"Taniguchi, Akira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshinobu","familyName":"Hagiwara","name":"Hagiwara, Yoshinobu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lotfi El","familyName":"Hafi","name":"Hafi, Lotfi El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tadahiro","familyName":"Taniguchi","name":"Taniguchi, Tadahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Ownership Understanding of Objects: Active Question Generation with Large Language Model and Probabilistic Generative Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:15:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:29:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robots operating in domestic and office environments must understand object ownership to correctly execute instructions such as ``Bring me my cup.'' However, ownership cannot be reliably inferred from visual features alone. To address this gap, we propose Active Ownership Learning (ActOwL), a framework that enables robots to actively generate and ask ownership-related questions to users. ActOwL employs a probabilistic generative model to select questions that maximize information gain, thereby acquiring ownership knowledge efficiently to improve learning efficiency. Additionally, by leveraging commonsense knowledge from Large Language Models (LLM), objects are pre-classified as either shared or owned, and only owned objects are targeted for questioning. Through experiments in a simulated home environment and a real-world laboratory setting, ActOwL achieved significantly higher ownership clustering accuracy with fewer questions than baseline methods. These findings demonstrate the effectiveness of combining active inference with LLM-guided commonsense reasoning, advancing the capability of robots to acquire ownership knowledge for practical and socially appropriate task execution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to AROB-ISBC 2026 (Journal Track option)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12754","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:43Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:47:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12753","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12753","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12753","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Warsaw University of Technology, Faculty of Electronics,Information Technology"],"givenName":"Feliks","familyName":"Bańka","name":"Bańka, Feliks","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Warsaw University of Technology"],"givenName":"Jarosław A.","familyName":"Chudziak","name":"Chudziak, Jarosław A.","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeltaHedge: A Multi-Agent Framework for Portfolio Options Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Portfolio Management (q-fin.PM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:14:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:29:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In volatile financial markets, balancing risk and return remains a significant challenge. Traditional approaches often focus solely on equity allocation, overlooking the strategic advantages of options trading for dynamic risk hedging. This work presents DeltaHedge, a multi-agent framework that integrates options trading with AI-driven portfolio management. By combining advanced reinforcement learning techniques with an ensembled options-based hedging strategy, DeltaHedge enhances risk-adjusted returns and stabilizes portfolio performance across varying market conditions. Experimental results demonstrate that DeltaHedge outperforms traditional strategies and standalone models, underscoring its potential to transform practical portfolio management in complex financial environments. Building on these findings, this paper contributes to the fields of quantitative finance and AI-driven portfolio optimization by introducing a novel multi-agent system for integrating options trading strategies, addressing a gap in the existing literature."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at Pacific Asia Conference on Information Systems (PACIS 2025), Kuala Lumpur. Official proceedings available at https://aisel.aisnet.org/pacis2025/aiandml/aiandml/25/. 16 pages, 7 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12753","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:42Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:47:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12750","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12750","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12750","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishab","familyName":"Parthasarathy","name":"Parthasarathy, Rishab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jasmine","familyName":"Collins","name":"Collins, Jasmine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cory","familyName":"Stephenson","name":"Stephenson, Cory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Makes a Good Generated Image? Investigating Human and Multimodal LLM Image Preference Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:10:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:28:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated evaluation of generative text-to-image models remains a challenging problem. Recent works have proposed using multimodal LLMs to judge the quality of images, but these works offer little insight into how multimodal LLMs make use of concepts relevant to humans, such as image style or composition, to generate their overall assessment. In this work, we study what attributes of an image--specifically aesthetics, lack of artifacts, anatomical accuracy, compositional correctness, object adherence, and style--are important for both LLMs and humans to make judgments on image quality. We first curate a dataset of human preferences using synthetically generated image pairs. We use inter-task correlation between each pair of image quality attributes to understand which attributes are related in making human judgments. Repeating the same analysis with LLMs, we find that the relationships between image quality attributes are much weaker. Finally, we study individual image quality attributes by generating synthetic datasets with a high degree of control for each axis. Humans are able to easily judge the quality of an image with respect to all of the specific image quality attributes (e.g. high vs. low aesthetic image), however we find that some attributes, such as anatomical accuracy, are much more difficult for multimodal LLMs to learn to judge. Taken together, these findings reveal interesting differences between how humans and multimodal LLMs perceive images."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 9 figures, 3 tables; appendix 16 pages, 9 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12750","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:38Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:47:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12744","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12744","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12744","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tusheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$L^2$-solutions to stochastic reaction-diffusion equations with superlinear drifts driven by space-time white noise^"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:00:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:28:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Consider the following stochastic reaction-diffusion equation with logarithmic superlinear coefficient b, driven by space-time white noise W: $$ u_t(t,x) = (1/2)u_{xx}(t,x) + b(u(t,x)) + σ(u(t,x))W(dt,dx) $$ for $t \u0026gt; 0$ and $x \\in [0,1]$, with initial condition $$ u(0,x) = u_0(x) $$ for $x \\in [0,1]$, where $u_0 \\in L^2[0,1]$.\n In this paper, we establish existence and uniqueness of probabilistically strong solutions in $C(R_+, L^2[0,1])$. Our result resolves a problem from [Ann. Probab. 47 (2019) 519-559] and provides an alternative proof of the non-blowup of $L^2[0,1]$ solutions from the same reference. We use new Gronwall-type inequalities. Due to nonlinearity, we work with first order moments, requiring precise estimates of the stochastic convolution."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12744","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:47:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":281,"totalPages":12,"page":8},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=8\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=9\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29"}}